  "reviewed_by": "product owner review, recorded during Task 6",
  "note": "The approved disposition for every control label in the vendored corpus. This file is the oracle: a test reads it and never derives what it expects from `semantic_replay.KIND_DISPOSITIONS`, because comparing one Jobloom table to another proves they agree and not that either is right. Every `always_manual` entry states why. It must describe the corpus exactly — one entry per recorded control, none missing and none extra — because a test that walks only this file cannot notice a control that was never reviewed. Each entry pins the whole reviewed answer and not just its first half: a disposition on its own is not a review, since `contact.email` mapped to `contact.phone` is still a `fact`. `expected_domain`/`expected_family` name what `field_policy.classify` must make of the recorded wording, null where no domain rule may fire at all. `expected_target` means one of two things by design. For a field with no automatic path it is the stable reason the pause carries — `employer_defined_compensation_manual`, `referral_contact_requires_user`, `sponsorship_meaning_ambiguous`. For the `eeo.*` kinds it is the protected policy domain, `voluntary_eeo`, because entering that domain hands the field to the non-disclosure policy rather than to a fixed code: the outcome there depends on what the user registered and on what the control is, and may be a planned step and no pause at all.",
